---
description: How to turn a Google Stitch export into a React Native screen
globs: design/stitch/**,apps/mobile/src/features/**/ui/**
alwaysApply: false
---

The export (`design/stitch/<ID>/code.html`, `screen.png`) is a picture with measurements. Use it like a designer's
spec, not like source code.

1. Start from `design/stitch/<ID>/notes.md`: it names the owner feature, the file to create and the route.
2. Decompose the screenshot into existing primitives/patterns from `@design-system`
   (ClayCard, ClayButton, ClayInput, MethodTag, Text, …). List the decomposition before writing code.
3. Missing primitive? Propose it (name + props) and add it to `src/design-system/` first. Never inline a
   one-off clay style in a feature.
4. Never copy CSS, Tailwind classes, hex colors, px values or SVG from the HTML. Snap spacing to the 4px grid
   and map every color to a token.
5. Sample text in the export is fake. Bind real data from the feature's application hooks.
6. States: `<ID>b`, `<ID>c`… folders are the same component in another state. Match each.
7. Sheets/dialogs are components owned by the host screen, not navigation routes.
8. Autofill surfaces (S30–S33) are Kotlin, not RN: see `.claude/autofill-service.md`.
9. After building, fill the "Translation notes" in notes.md (primitives used, deviations and why).
